# Python coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' style rules to Python; follows PEP 8. Applies only to `*.py`/`*.pyi` files.

## Formatting and typing

- Format with the project's configured formatter (`ruff format` or `black`); do not hand-format around it.
- Type-hint every new or touched public function signature, including `Optional`/`| None`; do not add hints to untouched code in the same change.
- Prefer `from __future__ import annotations` in modules that predate PEP 604 over importing `Optional`/`Union` piecemeal.
- Use `pathlib.Path`, not `os.path` string joins, for new filesystem code.
- Use f-strings for interpolation, not `%`-formatting or `.format()`; logging calls keep lazy `%s` (see `rules/patterns.mdc`).
- Run `ruff check .` (or the project's configured equivalent) instead of hand-checking unused imports, shadowed builtins or import order; fix what it flags rather than adding a blanket `# noqa`.

## Naming and structure

- One public class or cohesive function group per module; split a module that outgrows one screen of unrelated public names.
- Private internals get a single leading underscore (`_helper`), not name-mangled double-underscore unless subclass name-clash protection is the intent.

## Errors and imports

- Raise a specific exception type, never a bare `except:` or `except Exception:` that swallows an unrelated failure.
- Absolute imports for anything crossing a package boundary; relative imports only within one package's own submodules.
- Group imports stdlib / third-party / local, alphabetized; run `isort`/`ruff --select I` when configured instead of hand-ordering.

## Docstrings

- Every public function, class and module gets a docstring; a one-line summary is enough when the signature is self-describing.
- Match the project's docstring convention (Google, NumPy or reST); do not introduce a second style in one file.
